\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_50\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"git add collab/yang-php/1794566777000-engineer-p7b-ticks-b.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md && git commit -m 'docs(yang.php): record partial tick gate results' && git push origin p7b-ticks\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"[p7b-ticks 411c2b08] docs(yang.php): record partial tick gate results\\n 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)\\nTo github.com:sonwh98/datomworld.git\\n   752359df..411c2b08  p7b-ticks -> p7b-ticks\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794566777000-engineer-p7b-ticks-b.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:97:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_51\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"P7-b is **incomplete**. I pushed the pending tick fixtures, oracle runner, and findings to `p7b-ticks` in commits `752359df` and `411c2b08`.\\n\\nThe PHP runtime integration remains unimplemented: `declare(ticks)` is still inert, and the callback registry, tick hook, statement-site selection, and include site-set handoff are outstanding. The four single-unit fixtures fail at the missing registration builtin; the linked include fixture timed out, so it has no valid red result yet. All fixture expectations remain `:oracle :pending`.\\n\\nThe shared safepoint JVM test passed, as did the individual bundled and linked Python tracing pins. Cljstyle and clj-kondo passed on the touched files. The combined PHP and Python gate runs timed out; no Docker oracle run was made.\\n\\nDetails and the red-first chronology are in the [findings](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p7b/collab/yang-php/1794566777000-engineer-p7b-ticks-b.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md).\"}}\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:19:(`php:8.3-cli` on shiva, banner `php-8.3.35 shiva 2026-10-25`).\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:37:  in place and its list is generated on shiva.\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:96:dependencies: statement-level ticks wait on safepoint slice 3 (section 12),\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1248:shiva, 2026-10-17) and are the fixture group `oracle-2026-10-17`, recorded\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1500:under a ticks profile only (P7-b).\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1608:| Modules    | `include`, `require`, `_once`; | section 11; ticks per section  |\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1611:|            | `declare(ticks)`               |                                |\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1938:- **Statement ticks** (`declare(ticks=N)`, `register_tick_function`) need\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1943:  P7-a (signals, no dependency) and P7-b (ticks, behind safepoint slice 3).\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1977:  values, scope sharing, the two missing-include stages); safepoints (tick\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2106:| P7-b  | ticks: `declare(ticks)`,         | tick counts equal PHP's for the   |\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2107:|       | `register_tick_function`; behind | statement fixture                 |\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2528:|                             | honestly refused; corners: section 17.2; ticks |\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2561:  findings (among them PHP reference ownership and the tick and linked-matrix\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:3:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’ll read the PHP tick design, staged fixtures, and repository workflow first, then implement and run the gates sequentially with generous timeouts.\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:6:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-php/1794566777000-engineer-p7b-ticks-b.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/orch-lane/1794700000000-architect-safepoint-slice3-design.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:7:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-php/1794566777000-engineer-p7b-ticks-b.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/orch-lane/1794700000000-architect-safepoint-slice3-design.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-11-13\\nCoding-Agent: codex\\nBranch: p7b-ticks\\nStatus: incomplete\\n\\nA. The predecessor's shared :after implementation remains unchanged at `src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc:153` (commit 6ed8f768).\\n\\nB. Not implemented. `declare(ticks)` remains inert (`src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3554`); registry, hook, site selection and linked include site-set handoff remain outstanding. No parity claim is made.\\n\\nC. Partial. Five hand-derived, oracle-pending expectations are in `test/yang/php/antlr/programs_p7b.cljc:9`. `test/yang/php/antlr/ticks_test.clj:10` is intentionally red while B is absent. Chronology: sequence was added and run first, producing expected ATTBT vs actual empty output. Loop-body, function-call and generator-resume were then added and run red together: expected ATAT, FT, ATBT respectively, each actual empty. These fail at the missing registration builtin and do not yet isolate count semantics. Include-handoff was initially run without a linked harness (invalid red); it was then moved to the linked-unit harness (`ticks_test.clj:17`) and rerun; both the combined 120-second run and a separate 90-second retry timed out. The include case therefore does not yet have a valid red result. All expectations await oracle confirmation.\\n\\nD. Partial. `src/dev/yang/php/oracle.clj` has a P7 prologue, multi-unit runner and `--p7` group. Its namespace and prologue load successfully; the actual Docker execution is deliberately not run. Its fold provenance remains :pending until the orchestrator records it.\\n\\nE. `cljstyle check` and `clojure -M:kondo --lint` on touched Clojure files passed (0 errors, 0 warnings). Focused JVM PHP/shared and Python safepoint, and PHP fast e2e/lowering/prelude runs were started with 120-second timeouts; see final update below for results. Cross-host checks not run. Docker not run.\\n\\nGaps: no design gap established. B is unfinished work, not a claimed spec gap.\\n\\nEnvironment: generated PHP parsers present. `clj-kondo` is not on PATH, but the repository's `:kondo` alias works. Existing untracked prompt and stdout log are user/session artifacts and were not altered.\\n\\nGate update: the combined PHP/shared safepoint, Python bundled/linked safepoint, and PHP lowering/prelude/e2e JVM commands each timed out after 120 seconds without a result; they were run concurrently and contended for resources. A sequential retry of `yang.safepoint-test` passed 9 tests / 20 assertions; a sequential JVM retry of Python `trace-basic-test` passed 1 test / 4 assertions. A sequential JVM retry of Python `linked-tracing-profile-test` passed 1 test / 3 assertions. The linked include red run timed out after 120 seconds and its isolated retry after 90 seconds; neither established a valid include red. The P7 oracle runner's multi-unit preparation and working directory were checked with a mocked `clojure.java.shell/sh`; no Docker ran. Commit `752359df` was pushed. Its tests remain deliberately failing while B is absent.\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-05 0x:xx +0700\\nCoding-Agent: codex (gpt-6.1-sol, architect design — safepoint slice 3)\\n\\nI’ll read the architecture rules, section 12, and the current safepoint code, then develop a shared `:line` design for PHP ticks and Python tracing. I’ll keep this read-only and cite the relevant file locations.\\n\\n---\\n\\nThe shared `:line` extension is already in this checkout, along with Python tracing hooks and tests. The PHP section still describes it as pending. I’ll distinguish the existing contract from the PHP integration that remains, including whether a shared “current line” API needs anything beyond the hook operand already implemented.\\n\\n---\\n\\nThe main semantic issue is tick timing: the shared `:line` hook runs before its marked node, while Zend counts ticks after executing selected statements. A counter at every statement start would get callback order wrong. I’m checking how PHP lowering can preserve that timing while reusing the shared insertion primitive.\\n\\n---\\n\\nI’ve confirmed that include units need an explicit site-set handoff: canonical published rows carry no marks. I’ll make that a landing requirement for PHP, alongside a small profile option for hooks that run after normal statement completion. Python will keep its existing hook-before behavior, and neither path needs a VM semantic change.\\n\\n---\\n\\nThe shared `:line` mechanism already exists in this checkout. The remaining design work is PHP integration and one timing extension: Python needs a hook **before** a statement; PHP ticks need a hook **after normal completion**. Both can use the same marks, literal-operand API, and derivation stage.\\n\\nThis is a proposed design, based on source inspection. No files were edited and no tests were run.\\n\\n**1. Existing contract and proposed extension**\\n\\nThe PHP dependency statement is stale: [yang.php.md:1938](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:1938) says non-lambda sites are refused, but the implementation already defines `statement-kinds #{:line}` and sequences the hook before any marked node. See [safepoint.cljc:85](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc:85) and [safepoint.cljc:172](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc:172). Python tracing has bundled and linked profiles, and the design records its landing at `a822e152`: [yang.antlr.md:4645](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:4645).\\n\\nNo separate `docs/design/yang.python.md` is present. Python’s relevant specification is §8.5.2 of `yang.antlr.md`.\\n\\nRetain the existing mark model:\\n\\n- A `:line` mark belongs to the **occurrence of the lowered statement node**, including its statement cleanup.\\n- It does not belong to every expression, instruction, physical newline, or lambda.\\n- Separate statements on the same source line remain separate sites.\\n- A closure’s construction statement and statements inside its body have separate occurrences.\\n- Prelude code and generated implementation machinery remain unmarked.\\n\\nThis follows [yang.antlr.md:1596](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:1596). PHP’s existing temporary-cleanup boundary is particularly relevant: [lower.cljc:2279](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:2279).\\n\\nAdd one **proposed**, language-neutral profile option:\\n\\n```clojure\\n;; Existing behavior: before the marked node.\\n{:line {:hook 'py.sp/tline :operands true}}\\n\\n;; Proposed behavior: after the marked node completes normally.\\n{:line {:hook 'php.sp/line :operands true :after true}}\\n```\\n\\nThe default remains hook-before. `:after true` is valid only for statement sites; invalid combinations should fail during derivation.\\n\\nThe reason is observable PHP behavior. Zend emits tick instructions following statement compilation, excludes particular statement categories, and suppresses adjacent duplicate tick instructions. Consequently, counting every statement entry is insufficient for PHP parity. PHP lowering must select the corresponding completion sites, including declaration and control-flow cases. [PHP 8.3 compiler](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_compile.c)\\n\\nFor a marked node `S`, the two rewrites are conceptually:\\n\\n```clojure\\n;; Before: existing.\\n(do (hook literal-operands...) S)\\n\\n;; After: proposed. Preserve S's value mechanically.\\n(let [yang.safepoint/value S]\\n  (do (hook literal-operands...)\\n      yang.safepoint/value))\\n```\\n\\nBoth lower entirely to ordinary applications and lambdas. The after-hook runs once on normal completion, including completion after a suspension resumes. An escape bypassing completion skips it. It is not a `finally` hook.\\n\\nMarks must not overwrite existing `:call` or `:loop` marks. PHP should mark the outer statement/cleanup node while preserving inner call sites and loop lambdas. The current representation holds one mark per path; adding multiple marks per occurrence is outside this slice. See [core/lower.cljc:76](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/core/lower.cljc:76).\\n\\n**2. Writer and reader API**\\n\\nUse the existing metadata and literal-operand API, without introducing a VM “current line” register.\\n\\n| Operation | Contract |\\n|---|---|\\n| Frontend writes a site | `(core/site [:line n ...literal-data] statement-node)` |\\n| Projection transports it | `[origin root path :yang/site mark]` |\\n| Stage reads sites | `(safepoint/sites metadata origin root)` |\\n| Composition selects behavior | Profile entry for `:line` |\\n| Runtime consumer reads the line | First literal argument passed to its hook |\\n\\nThese APIs already exist: [core/lower.cljc:76](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/core/lower.cljc:76), [yin/vm.cljc:1217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1217), and [safepoint.cljc:96](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc:96).\\n\\nRecommended consumer shapes:\\n\\n```clojure\\n;; Python: existing.\\n[:line n]\\n;; -> (py.sp/tline n)\\n\\n;; PHP: proposed payload; generic insertion does not interpret the map.\\n[:line n {:unit unit-path :ticks N}]\\n;; -> (php.sp/line n {:unit unit-path :ticks N})\\n```\\n\\nPHP receives the unit explicitly because a closure can execute after its defining include has returned. Reading only the currently including unit would misidentify its source.\\n\\nThe line-number conventions remain frontend-owned:\\n\\n- PHP uses its packet’s physical-line side table; existing code returns `0` when unavailable: [lower.cljc:3298](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3298).\\n- Python currently uses logical lines counted from `NEWLINE` tokens. This slice preserves that documented limitation: [lower.cljc:198](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:198).\\n\\nThe PHP tick consumer uses the supplied `N` and a request-owned counter. Increment at each selected completion; when the counter reaches that site’s threshold, reset it before dispatch. Counter state survives calls, includes, and generator switches. Zend likewise uses one execution counter with the threshold carried by each tick instruction. [PHP 8.3 tick handler](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_vm_def.h)\\n\\nKeep this state separate from:\\n\\n- The execution-budget counter, currently named `php.sp/tick`: [PHP safepoint.cljc:23](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/safepoint.cljc:23).\\n- Escape-restored dynamic context.\\n- Source-location data.\\n\\nPHP callback dispatch needs its own semantics. In particular, suppression is **per executing registered handler**, rather than Python’s global tracing-busy flag. Hook operands are not additional arguments to registered PHP callbacks. [PHP tick callback implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/ext/standard/basic_functions.c)\\n\\nPython already consumes the same primitive: `py.sp/tline` receives `n`, finds the running function’s frame, writes `f_lineno`, and invokes its local trace function. See [Python safepoint.cljc:265](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/safepoint.cljc:265). Its tracing state and frame restoration remain Python-owned.\\n\\nThus “current line” means the location supplied to the current hook. A consumer may retain that location in its own frame or context. There is no ambient cross-language mutable location.\\n\\n**3. Mark lifecycle and linked retention**\\n\\nMarks are static occurrence data. Executing a mark does not consume or remove it.\\n\\n| Boundary | Required behavior |\\n|---|---|\\n| Include unit | Retain that unit’s own statement locations and lexical tick thresholds. Sharing caller variables does not share its source map. Repeated execution revisits its sites; `_once` skips the body when appropriate. |\\n| Eval unit, P9 | A new compilation produces its own source occurrence, site set, and derivation under the run’s selected profile. Caller scope sharing does not substitute caller locations. Until P9, preserve the existing unsupported result. |\\n| Generator suspension | Instrumented code and the pending continuation survive suspension. A before-hook is not replayed merely by resuming; a pending after-hook runs when its statement eventually completes normally. |\\n| Closure | Its body retains defining-unit locations and compile-time tick thresholds. Invocation uses the invoking request’s runtime tick state. Creating a closure does not execute its body’s sites. |\\n| Exception/escape | Static sites remain available. Frame/location restoration follows language context restoration; the request’s tick counter is not rolled back. |\\n\\nRelevant existing boundaries are [yang.php.md:1875](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:1875), [yang.php.md:360](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:360), and [PHP prelude.cljc:4506](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4506). PHP already saves generator context at yield and restores the caller context: [prelude.cljc:4538](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4538).\\n\\nThis guarantees location retention, not complete CPython generator-tracing parity. Python’s existing call mark surrounds function invocation, including generator construction: [Python lower.cljc:1482](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:1482). Generator trace-event policy needs separate acceptance before broader parity is claimed.\\n\\nThe linker retention contract is:\\n\\n| Artifact | Retain |\\n|---|---|\\n| Canonical program `A` | Original rows, without line literals or mark fields |\\n| Published source association | Content-addressed occurrence site set, bound to the exact canonical root and frontend/profile revision |\\n| Derived executable `A′` | Selected hook applications and their literal operands |\\n| Derivation record | Input/output roots, complete hook profile—including `:after`—and selected-site-set address |\\n| Source archive | Original occurrence metadata and positions needed to recover provenance |\\n\\nThis matches the existing separation between canonical publication and derived execution: [yang.antlr.md:1613](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:1613). Derived envelopes intentionally contain empty side tables: [safepoint.cljc:234](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc:234).\\n\\nA published canonical tree alone cannot recover marks. Python addresses this through published site sets and link-time derivation: [yang.antlr.md:3040](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3040). **PHP does not yet publish them**—its publication configuration explicitly sets `:site-set nil`: [publication.cljc:41](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/publication.cljc:41).\\n\\nTherefore PHP’s linked ticks profile must:\\n\\n- Publish and retrieve verified site sets for included guest code.\\n- Require the appropriate hook module and rebind dependencies to derived images.\\n- Refuse missing or invalid site metadata when ticks are required; it must not silently run that dependency without ticks.\\n- Pin the selected source/site association explicitly when multiple source occurrences share canonical rows.\\n\\nFor include cycles, instrument the SCC bundle and repin member aliases to that derived bundle. The existing publisher places marks on bundles, not aliases: [core/publication.cljc:147](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/core/publication.cljc:147). Python currently documents an SCC admission limitation; copying that limitation would not establish PHP’s cyclic-include acceptance.\\n\\n**Stripping rule:** omit `:line` from the profile to derive an executable without line hooks. Never delete inserted calls from an already derived image. A compact execution package may omit side tables after derivation, provided its pinned provenance remains retrievable. A package intended for later instrumentation must retain/retrieve its site set.\\n\\nThere is no automatic hook-prelude dead-code stripping promised here. Python’s current module emitter includes its definition list regardless of selected profile: [Python safepoint.cljc:425](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/safepoint.cljc:425).\\n\\n**4. Cost and host deltas**\\n\\nAt an executed selected site, excluding consumer work:\\n\\n| Placement | Added application occurrences | Added lambdas |\\n|---|---:|---:|\\n| Before | 2 | 1 |\\n| After, value preserving | 3 | 2 |\\n| `:line` omitted | 0 | 0 |\\n\\nThe existing before shape is visible at [safepoint.cljc:128](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc:128). Runtime cell accesses, polling, frame updates, and guest calls are additional consumer costs; “one cell read per statement” is not a sufficient description of the current Python tracing hook.\\n\\nDerivation traverses the program and recomputes tail marks; sorted site-set construction adds sorting work. Bundle growth includes wrappers, operands, changed ancestors, and tail-mark changes. Content addressing shares identical rows, so these occurrence counts are not byte estimates. Measure rows and serialized bytes in acceptance.\\n\\n**No `yin.vm` semantic change is required.** No new opcode, effect, semantic-contract stamp, UCF field, or host location register is introduced.\\n\\nLanguage instrumentation hooks are ordinary calls. They are distinct from the VM’s parking/migration safepoints, whose table concerns blocked operations, explicit parks, and halt: [yin.vm.semantic.md:655](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:655).\\n\\nThe after-wrapper needs a distinct reserved result binder and tail recomputation. Its continuation uses existing machinery. Application placement alone adds no host effect capability.\\n\\n**5. Acceptance and landing contract**\\n\\nUse this PHP fixture to expose both count and callback ordering:\\n\\n```php\\n<?php\\nfunction tick_handler() { echo \\\"T\\\"; }\\nregister_tick_function('tick_handler');\\n\\ndeclare(ticks=1) {\\n    echo \\\"A\\\";\\n    $x = 1;\\n    echo \\\"B\\\";\\n}\\n\\nunregister_tick_function('tick_handler');\\n```\\n\\nProposed oracle assertions:\\n\\n| Threshold | Output | Handler calls |\\n|---|---|---:|\\n| `1` | `ATTBT` | 3 |\\n| `2` | `ATB` | 1 |\\n| `0` | `AB` | 0 |\\n\\nRecord these against the project’s pinned PHP oracle before accepting them as goldens; the repository records PHP `8.3.35` oracle provenance at [programs.cljc:10](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/php/antlr/programs.cljc:10).\\n\\nPHP acceptance must additionally cover:\\n\\n- Same-line statements and non-tickable expression subparts.\\n- Nested directives, threshold changes, and adjacent-tick suppression.\\n- Registration/unregistration inside ticked code and handler exceptions.\\n- Temporary destruction before the completion callback.\\n- Return, throw, break, continue, and `finally`.\\n- Included units with and without their own directives; `_once`; include cycles.\\n- Closures invoked after their defining include returns.\\n- Two generators interleaved across a yield, proving no replay and one shared request counter.\\n- Unchanged execution-budget charging: adding `:line` must not charge the `:loop`/`:call` budget.\\n\\nFor Python, retain the existing basic trace fixture and golden:\\n\\n```text\\ncall f 7\\nline f 8\\ncall g 5\\nline g 6\\nreturn g 6\\nline f 9\\nreturn f 9\\n```\\n\\nIt already exists in bundled and linked tests: [safepoint_test.cljc:805](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/python/antlr/safepoint_test.cljc:805), [linked_safepoint_test.cljc:401](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:401). Preserve unwind restoration, callback suppression, newline handling, and unsupported-profile tests.\\n\\nDo not enlarge Python’s parity claim: the documented slice excludes exception/C-call events, module-level events, and certain header events: [yang.antlr.md:1770](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:1770).\\n\\nThe **shared landing gate** is:\\n\\n- Before and after placement work on non-lambda nodes, preserving values and evaluation order.\\n- Escapes skip after-hooks; suspension/resumption neither loses nor duplicates them.\\n- Empty selection remains identity; canonical input remains unchanged.\\n- Existing Python derivation goldens remain unchanged.\\n- Derivation is deterministic across JVM, Node, and Dart.\\n- All four evaluators pass applicable execution fixtures.\\n- Prelude exclusion, tail behavior, provenance joins, and fail-closed hook resolution hold.\\n\\nPassing that gate unblocks PHP P7-b. P7-b itself remains incomplete until its tick semantics and linked-unit acceptance pass. Eval execution fixtures remain P9 work.\\n\\n**6. Implementation slices, seats, and ordering**\\n\\n| Order | Seat | Files and work |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Architect + safepoint owner | Reconcile stale status in `docs/design/yang.php.md` §12 and `docs/design/yang.antlr.md` §8.5.2; specify `:after`, payloads, retention, and acceptance. |\\n| 2 | Shared safepoint compiler seat | `src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc`: add validated after-placement, value-preserving rewrite, and provenance-path handling. Reserve the result binder in `src/cljc/yang/core/names.cljc`. Add focused cases to `test/yang/safepoint_test.cljc`. |\\n| 3 | QA/verification seat | Run shared cross-host derivation checks and existing Python bundled/linked tracing regressions. Establish the shared landing gate. |\\n| 4 | PHP compiler/runtime seat | `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc`: lexical directive tracking and completion-site selection. `safepoint.cljc`: distinct statement-tick hooks and bundled/linked emission. `prelude.cljc`: request-owned state and builtin wiring. Extend PHP safepoint fixtures. |\\n| 5 | Publication/linker integration seat | Enable the existing publication `:site-set` seam in PHP; add a PHP site-set adapter, verified link-time derivation, hook dependency pins, and SCC alias rebinding. Extend include and portable publication fixtures. |\\n| 6 | QA + independent reviewer | Record pinned PHP oracle results; verify consumer semantics, lifecycle, bundle sizes, and the required host/evaluator lanes. Review shared and PHP completion separately. |\\n| Later | P9 eval seat | Feed each admitted eval compilation through the same site/profiling contract and add eval-boundary fixtures. |\\n\\nThe shared insertion change belongs to the safepoint seat, as PHP explicitly requires at [yang.php.md:1944](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.php.md:1944). Python needs regression coverage for this work; its existing tracing implementation does not need to be rebuilt.\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:8:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat test/yang/php/antlr/ticks_test.clj test/yang/php/antlr/programs_p7b.cljc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:9:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat test/yang/php/antlr/ticks_test.clj test/yang/php/antlr/programs_p7b.cljc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"(ns yang.php.antlr.ticks-test\\n  (:require\\n    [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.cst :as cst]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.e2e-harness :as h]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.linked-harness :as lh]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.programs-p7b :as p7b]))\\n\\n\\n(deftest tick-fixtures-test\\n  (doseq [{:keys [id source out units]} p7b/fixtures\\n          :when (nil? units)]\\n    (testing (name id)\\n      (is (= out (:php/out (h/run-raw (str \\\"<?php \\\" p7b/prologue source))))))))\\n\\n\\n(deftest include-handoff-test\\n  (let [{:keys [source units out]} (last p7b/fixtures)\\n        packets (update-vals (assoc units \\\"main.php\\\" (str \\\"<?php \\\" p7b/prologue source))\\n                             cst/parse-source)\\n        result (lh/run-fixture packets \\\"main.php\\\" {})]\\n    (doseq [[vm value] (:values result)]\\n      (is (= out (h/latin1->text (:php/out value))) (name vm)))))\\n(ns yang.php.antlr.programs-p7b\\n  \\\"Hand-derived tick expectations pending the pinned PHP oracle pass.\\\")\\n\\n\\n(def prologue\\n  \\\"function tick_handler() { echo 'T'; } register_tick_function('tick_handler'); \\\")\\n\\n\\n(def fixtures\\n  [{:id :sequence, :source \\\"declare(ticks=1) { echo 'A'; $x = 1; echo 'B'; }\\\",\\n    :out \\\"ATTBT\\\", :oracle :pending}\\n   {:id :loop-body, :source \\\"for ($i=0; $i<2; $i++) { declare(ticks=1) { echo 'A'; } }\\\",\\n    :out \\\"ATAT\\\", :oracle :pending}\\n   {:id :function-call, :source \\\"function f() { echo 'F'; } declare(ticks=1) { f(); }\\\",\\n    :out \\\"FT\\\", :oracle :pending}\\n   {:id :generator-resume,\\n    :source \\\"function g() { declare(ticks=1) { yield 1; echo 'B'; } } $g=g(); $g->current(); echo 'A'; $g->next();\\\",\\n    :out \\\"ATBT\\\", :oracle :pending}\\n   {:id :include-handoff, :source \\\"include 'lib.php';\\\",\\n    :units {\\\"lib.php\\\" \\\"<?php declare(ticks=1) { echo 'L'; }\\\"},\\n    :out \\\"LT\\\", :oracle :pending}])\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:10:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'ticks|declareStatement|declare\\\\\\\\(' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/{lower,prelude,safepoint,publication}.cljc test/yang/php/antlr/ticks_test.clj docs/design/yang.php.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:11:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'ticks|declareStatement|declare\\\\\\\\(' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/{lower,prelude,safepoint,publication}.cljc test/yang/php/antlr/ticks_test.clj docs/design/yang.php.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"test/yang/php/antlr/ticks_test.clj:1:(ns yang.php.antlr.ticks-test\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:96:dependencies: statement-level ticks wait on safepoint slice 3 (section 12),\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:286:  the coercion rules, with `TypeError` under `declare(strict_types=1)` (a\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1427:`declare(encoding)`; `__halt_compiler`; `eval` until its slice; `$$` and\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1481:`echoStatement`, `inlineHtmlStatement`, `declareStatement`, `gotoStatement`,\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1500:under a ticks profile only (P7-b).\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1608:| Modules    | `include`, `require`, `_once`; | section 11; ticks per section  |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1610:|            | `declare(strict_types)`,       |                                |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1611:|            | `declare(ticks)`               |                                |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1618:|            | `declare(encoding)`; nested    |                                |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1938:- **Statement ticks** (`declare(ticks=N)`, `register_tick_function`) need\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1943:  P7-a (signals, no dependency) and P7-b (ticks, behind safepoint slice 3).\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2106:| P7-b  | ticks: `declare(ticks)`,         | tick counts equal PHP's for the   |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2528:|                             | honestly refused; corners: section 17.2; ticks |\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:92:   \\\"declareStatement\\\" :declare,\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:452:              (let [declare? (p/rule? (first (core/rule-kids ctx head)) \\\"declareStatement\\\")]\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3555:  \\\"`declare(strict_types=1)` switches the unit to strict scalar typing; `ticks`\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3612:    \\\"declareStatement\\\" (lower-declare ctx n)\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:12:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'p7b|ticks' collab --glob '*brief*'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:13:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'p7b|ticks' collab --glob '*brief*'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"collab/general/1790523957004-architect-shibi-cap-vs-currency-brief.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:24:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_11\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '185,225p' docs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\\n### 6.2 What the option carries\\\\n\\\\n```text\\\\n:link-policy {:lease {:duration {:seconds 30}\\\\n                      :tolerance {:seconds 2}\\\\n                      :max {:seconds 300}        ; optional cap\\\\n                      :ticks tick-source}}       ; host adapter\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nUnits are the composition's interoperability decision, fixed once. The\\\\nsizing relations of `dao.lease.md` hold: the duration exceeds twice the\\\\nrenewal interval, and the tolerance covers flight time and the rate skew\\\\nbetween the judge's and the holder's tick streams.\\\\n\\\\n### 6.3 Attribution comes by construction\\\\n\\\\n`dao.lease.md` makes attribution a composition duty, and warns that\\\\nin-process media distinguish no writers unless the composition arranges it.\\\\nHere it can be arranged completely: the lease media and the tick streams\\\\nare the shell's own, per-author, and never placed in a VM's private\\\\n`:resources` or handed to any program value. A program holds only sealed\\\\nreferences to resources the engine issued, so it cannot append a forged\\\\ngrant or renewal. The resolver is therefore the shell's own knowledge of\\\\nwhich medium each author writes. A capability token such as ShiBi is not\\\\nneeded for this case and would add nothing until leases guard something\\\\nthat untrusted writers can reach.\\\\n\\\\n### 6.4 What phase 2 costs\\\\n\\\\n- a tick adapter per host (a timer on the JVM, on Node and on Dart), which\\\\n  is the one place a host clock is read, outside the REPL core;\\\\n- a judge, driven at a declared cadence by the same host driver that\\\\n  drives the shell;\\\\n- lease bookkeeping that survives the rollbacks M5 fixed: the lease for a\\\\n  link is reclaimed by the same abandon path, and a link identity is never\\\\n  reused on the surviving pair;\\\\n- scripted-tick tests, as `dao.lease`'s own tests are: no test reads a\\\\n  real clock;\\\\n- process-scoped leases only. The judge's ledger is not rebuildable from\\\\n  any stream and the resource is not durable, so a restart drops the\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/dao-stream/1790497500000-architect-pending-link-policy-brief.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:9:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n -C 8 'pending|lease|deadline|expire|timeout' docs/design/dao.lease.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"1-# DaoLease\\\\n2-\\\\n3:Status: implemented (`src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc`, `test/dao/lease_test.cljc`,\\\\n4:`test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc`; merged 2026-09-20), derived from and\\\\n5-subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). The three use cases in\\\\n6:[`dao.lease.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.lease.implementation-plan.md) are\\\\n7-sketches, not finished components. This document is the operative contract:\\\\n8-every sentence below is a rule. Why the design is this shape is in\\\\n9:[`dao.lease.rationale.md`](./dao.lease.rationale.md), which binds nothing.\\\\n10-\\\\n11:A lease is a grant that lapses unless renewed. It adds no operation to any\\\\n12-contract and no key to any DaoStream result map, leaving\\\\n13-[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) unchanged by its existence.\\\\n14-\\\\n15-## Vocabulary\\\\n16-\\\\n17-Facts are plain data on ordinary streams, classified by two dispatch keys:\\\\n18:`:dao.lease/status` for the negotiation, `:dao.lease/event` for evidence. A\\\\n19:reader switches on both. A fact carrying neither is not a lease fact and is\\\\n20-ignored; a fact carrying both is defective.\\\\n21-\\\\n22-+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n23:| Fact     | `:dao.lease/status`   | Author  | Identity carried                            | Also required                                                             |\\\\n24-+==========+=======================+=========+=============================================+===========================================================================+\\\\n25:| Proposal | `:dao.lease/proposed` | holder  | `:dao.lease/proposal`                       | `:dao.lease/subject`; `:dao.lease/duration` optional, an ask              |\\\\n26-+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n27:| Grant    | `:dao.lease/accepted` | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`, plus                    | `:dao.lease/subject`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:dao.lease/duration`;         |\\\\n28:|          |                       |         | `:dao.lease/proposal` if answering one      | `:dao.lease/max` optional                                                 |\\\\n29-+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n30:| Refusal  | `:dao.lease/rejected` | grantor | `:dao.lease/proposal`                       | —                                                                         |\\\\n31-+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n32:| Release  | `:dao.lease/released` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`                          | —                                                                         |\\\\n33-+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n34:| Reclaim  | `:dao.lease/lapsed`   | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`                          | `:dao.lease/cause` — `:silence`, `:release`, `:cap`, or `:policy`         |\\\\n35-+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n36-\\\\n37-+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\\\\n38:| Evidence   | `:dao.lease/event`   | Author  | Identity carried     | Also required        |\\\\n39-+============+======================+=========+======================+======================+\\\\n40:| Renewal    | `:dao.lease/renewal` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`   | —                    |\\\\n41-+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\\\\n42:| Tick       | `:dao.lease/tick`    | adapter | —                    | `:dao.lease/reading` |\\\\n43-+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\\\\n44-\\\\n45-**Keys.**\\\\n46-\\\\n47:- `:dao.lease/lease` — the lease's identity, minted by the grantor, carried by\\\\n48-  every fact about it, never reused within that grantor.\\\\n49:- `:dao.lease/proposal` — a proposal's identity, minted by the holder, echoed\\\\n50-  by the grant or refusal answering it.\\\\n51:- `:dao.lease/subject` — what is leased, in whatever plain data the domain uses.\\\\n52:- `:dao.lease/holder` — the party whose renewals count, in whatever form the\\\\n53-  medium's attribution takes.\\\\n54:- `:dao.lease/duration` — a single-entry map from a unit keyword to a positive\\\\n55:  integer magnitude. `:dao.lease/max`, `:dao.lease/reading` and a judge's\\\\n56-  tolerance use the same representation; tolerance may be zero. A magnitude\\\\n57-  stays within the bound that keeps every comparison and sum of the\\\\n58-  composition's units exact on every host — the reference bound is 2^52 raw\\\\n59-  and, against a supplied table, `quot 2^52 unit-magnitude`; a magnitude past\\\\n60-  it establishes nothing.\\\\n61:- `:dao.lease/cause` — why a reclaim happened.\\\\n62-\\\\n63-**Units.** The unit keywords and their ratios are a composition's\\\\n64-interoperability decision, fixed once and shared by its grantor, holders and\\\\n65-judge. Comparison normalizes to the finer of two units, and is strict: an\\\\n66-interval exactly equal to its bound has not yet passed it.\\\\n67-\\\\n68-**Validity.** A fact is defective, and establishes nothing, when it omits a key\\\\n69-its status or event requires; carries a status or event outside the tables\\\\n70-above; carries a duration, cap or reading that is not a single-entry map of a\\\\n71:fixed unit to a positive integer; carries a lease id on a proposal; answers one\\\\n72-proposal with both `:accepted` and `:rejected`, or repeats `:accepted`,\\\\n73:`:released` or `:lapsed` for one lease; or carries a reading older than one\\\\n74:already observed. A judge reading a defective fact has read no lease fact.\\\\n75-\\\\n76-## Authority\\\\n77-\\\\n78-- **Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot grant itself\\\\n79-  tenure. A holder fact establishes no term; a delayed holder renewal is still\\\\n80-  evidence and counts as such.\\\\n81:- **A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.**\\\\n82-  A renewal from any other author is a fact about that author, and silence is\\\\n83-  measured as though it had not arrived.\\\\n84-- **At most one of `:accepted` or `:rejected` answers a proposal.**\\\\n85-  Renegotiating is a new proposal with a new proposal identity.\\\\n86-- **A proposal creates no state.** Nothing exists until `:accepted`. A grantor\\\\n87-  owes no answer, and a holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by\\\\n88-  nothing in this vocabulary.\\\\n89-- **A grantor may grant unsolicited**, with no proposal, in which case the\\\\n90-  holder is the party the grant is delivered to.\\\\n91-- **A `:policy` cause may not consist of declining to count evidence\\\\n92-  received.** Eligible renewals are counted whether or not another condition\\\\n93:  ends the lease.\\\\n94-\\\\n95-## Prohibitions\\\\n96-\\\\n97:- A lease may not extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no\\\\n98:  DaoStream operation may consult a lease.\\\\n99-- No timer holds a callback and no notification marks a lapse.\\\\n100-- No absolute time appears in any fact.\\\\n101-- A reclaim frees the resource, never the record.\\\\n102-- A holder renews from its own control flow. No machinery renews on its behalf.\\\\n103-\\\\n104-## Time\\\\n105-\\\\n106:Time reaches lease code only as data; neither judge nor holder reads a host\\\\n107-clock. Both read a tick stream their composition wires. Readings on one stream\\\\n108-never decrease, and a composition persisting a ledger across a restart keeps\\\\n109-its readings comparable across it.\\\\n110-\\\\n111:A tick-stream gap makes a pass late, not wrong; a lease-fact gap is governed\\\\n112-under *Judging with incomplete evidence*.\\\\n113-\\\\n114-## The judge\\\\n115-\\\\n116-The judge is composed **inside the boundary that possesses the resource** and\\\\n117-reclaims by acting on what it itself holds; one that does not possess the\\\\n118-resource is outside this contract. It concludes and reclaims in one\\\\n119-interpreter, which is not the forbidden collapse: it is the possessing host\\\\n120-interpreter performing its own act.\\\\n121-\\\\n122:**Ledger.** The judge keeps, privately, for each live lease:\\\\n123-\\\\n124-- its **terms** — subject, holder, duration, cap;\\\\n125-- its **tenure start**, the reading the grant was seeded at, which no renewal\\\\n126-  changes;\\\\n127-- its **last relevant observation**, the reading of the grant or newest\\\\n128-  eligible renewal, whichever is later;\\\\n129-- its **evidence state** — `known`, or `unknown` with its resumed reading;\\\\n130:- its **reclaim state** — `live`, or `pending` carrying the classified cause\\\\n131-  and whether the reclaim succeeded.\\\\n132-\\\\n133:A lease **enters** the ledger when the grantor grants it, seeded from that act,\\\\n134-and **leaves** once its reclaim has succeeded and its `:lapsed` record has been\\\\n135-appended with `:dao.stream/ok`. Until both, it remains.\\\\n136-\\\\n137-The ledger is not rebuildable from any stream: a composition either persists it,\\\\n138-as a stream its owner writes and reads, or recovers by the rule under *Restart*.\\\\n139-\\\\n140-### The pass\\\\n141-\\\\n142-A pass classifies nothing until a first tick has been observed. At each pass,\\\\n143-in this order:\\\\n144-\\\\n145-1. **Drain every wired tick cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`. The newest\\\\n146-   reading drained, or the newest previously observed if none were drained, is\\\\n147-   **now** for the whole pass.\\\\n148:2. **Drain every wired lease-fact cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`, stamping\\\\n149-   each fact with that same *now*, and apply it: an eligible renewal advances\\\\n150:   the lease's last relevant observation to *now* and returns it to `known`; a\\\\n151:   valid `:released` from the holder marks it released; a `:dao.stream/gap`\\\\n152:   marks every lease on that medium `unknown` with *now* as its resumed\\\\n153-   reading. Each cursor's drain may be bounded by a composition-declared\\\\n154-   budget; a cursor still yielding when the budget is spent has not completed\\\\n155-   its observation, and while any wired fact cursor is so truncated, the pass\\\\n156:   classifies no `:silence` for the leases that truncated medium carries —\\\\n157:   `:release`, `:cap`, `:policy` and pending retries are never suppressed. An\\\\n158-   incomplete drain may delay a reclaim; it may never manufacture one.\\\\n159-3. **Answer and grant.** Proposals drained this pass may be answered, and\\\\n160-   unsolicited grants authored, both seeded and stamped with this pass's *now*.\\\\n161-   A grant authored between passes is seeded with the next pass's *now*.\\\\n162:4. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds, and the\\\\n163:   first that holds in this order is its cause: it is already `pending`, and\\\\n164:   keeps the cause it carries; a valid `:released` from its holder has been\\\\n165:   observed, `:release`; its tenure start is further back than\\\\n166:   `:dao.lease/max`, `:cap`; the grantor's own policy ends it, `:policy`; its\\\\n167-   interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus\\\\n168:   tolerance, `:silence`. A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for\\\\n169-   `:silence` only once, in addition, a full duration has passed since its\\\\n170-   resumed reading.\\\\n171:5. **Reclaim** each due lease by its composition-supplied procedure, marking it\\\\n172:   `pending` with its cause first. The procedure is idempotent and reports\\\\n173-   whether it succeeded; a reclaim that fails or does not report leaves the\\\\n174:   lease `pending` and unrecorded.\\\\n175:6. **Record** `:lapsed`, carrying the cause the ledger holds, for each lease\\\\n176:   whose reclaim succeeded. On `:dao.stream/ok` the lease leaves the ledger;\\\\n177:   otherwise it stays `pending` and the next pass reaches it again through the\\\\n178-   first clause of step 4.\\\\n179-\\\\n180-The record follows the act and never precedes it. **The absence of a `:lapsed`\\\\n181-fact is never evidence of tenure.**\\\\n182-\\\\n183-A drain that ends in `:dao.stream/end` retires that cursor for subsequent\\\\n184-passes. A drain that ends in `:dao.stream/transport-error` ends the pass before\\\\n185-step 4.\\\\n186-\\\\n187-### Judging with incomplete evidence\\\\n188-\\\\n189-Absence is evidence only over a window the judge observed. After a\\\\n190:`:dao.stream/gap`, a lease with no renewal in the retained tail is `unknown`,\\\\n191:and a surviving older renewal is not inferred to be the newest. A lease\\\\n192-recovered from a persisted ledger is `unknown`. Conditions other than\\\\n193:`:silence` apply to an `unknown` lease unchanged.\\\\n194-\\\\n195-### Restart\\\\n196-\\\\n197-A grantor that has lost its ledger **reclaims and re-grants**: for each\\\\n198-resource it still possesses it performs the reclaim first, then grants afresh.\\\\n199-The reclaim ends the prior tenure; a new grant alone does not.\\\\n200-\\\\n201-This requires an inventory of possessed resources and a recoverable holder for\\\\n--\\\\n208-  it by the same attribution its composition supplies to the judge. A holder\\\\n209-  that has not observed its grant holds nothing.\\\\n210-- **Renew at less than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own\\\\n211-  tick stream. A renewal is an append returning `:dao.stream/ok`; any other\\\\n212-  outcome does not advance the holder's bound.\\\\n213-- **Stop acting at the bound** — the earlier of the duration since the later of\\\\n214-  its last renewal and its observed grant, and the cap the grant carries —\\\\n215-  whether or not anything has been heard.\\\\n216:- **Release when done.** The grantor still performs the reclaim and records\\\\n217-  it.\\\\n218-- **Treat an unusable reading as unanswerable.** A reading that is not a\\\\n219-  single-entry `{unit, positive integer magnitude}` within the composition's\\\\n220-  table answers nothing: every holder discipline fails closed on it, and a\\\\n221-  flow that guards on its bound alone does not act.\\\\n222-\\\\n223-For cap purposes, the holder measures the cap interval from the reading at\\\\n224-which it observed the grant; that reading may be later than the grantor\\\\n--\\\\n226-extend, delay, or alter the judge's cap or reclaim decision; a resource\\\\n227-requiring exclusion must enforce it through fencing.\\\\n228-\\\\n229-The bound bounds attention, not access; a holder needing exclusion obtains it\\\\n230-from the resource.\\\\n231-\\\\n232-## Composition duties\\\\n233-\\\\n234:A composition that grants leases owes all of the following.\\\\n235-\\\\n236-- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime driving it at a declared cadence** —\\\\n237-  the maximum interval between *completed* passes.\\\\n238-- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for every holder.\\\\n239-- **A tolerance** supplied to the judge, possibly zero.\\\\n240-- **An attribution resolver** for the judge and for every holder observing a\\\\n241-  grant — its own answer to who authored a fact, by per-author media, an\\\\n242-  envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity, since DaoStream supplies\\\\n--\\\\n245-- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\\\\n246-- **A stream the grantor writes grants and `:lapsed` to**, and the medium each\\\\n247-  recipient reads its carried facts from.\\\\n248-- **Media that either retain without eviction or declare evict-oldest**,\\\\n249-  refused at assembly otherwise.\\\\n250-- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a\\\\n251-  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may\\\\n252-  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\\\\n253:  all three keeps its leases process-scoped.\\\\n254-\\\\n255-## Sizing\\\\n256-\\\\n257-Stated as relations; the values belong to compositions.\\\\n258-\\\\n259-- The granted duration exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.\\\\n260-- The judge's tolerance covers expected flight time and the rate skew between\\\\n261-  its tick stream and the holder's.\\\\n--\\\\n268-\\\\n269-## Limits\\\\n270-\\\\n271-- **A false lapse is possible.** Delay errs against the holder while a renewal\\\\n272-  is unobserved and for it once observed, so a late-observed renewal from a\\\\n273-  dead holder buys a fresh duration, and continuity is lost when none is\\\\n274-  observed before a completed pass finds the interval past duration plus\\\\n275-  tolerance. Partition length alone decides neither.\\\\n276:- **`:dao.lease/max` binds within one ledger lifetime.**\\\\n277-- **A medium that gaps more often than one duration** never completes an\\\\n278-  observation window and leaks continuously while every component reports\\\\n279-  healthy; per-attachment media are the isolation.\\\\n280-- **Reclamation is bounded by** duration plus tolerance plus cadence plus the\\\\n281-  reclaim's own cost, given an uninterrupted observation window.\\\\n282-\\\\n283-## Carriage\\\\n284-\\\\n285:Proposals, grants, refusals, releases and renewals cross a boundary as ordinary\\\\n286-payload, carried by the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage\\\\n287-is delivery, not a second authoring.\\\\n288-\\\\n289-`:lapsed` does not cross; it is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream. A\\\\n290-remote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a served connection, an\\\\n291-ordinary `:ws/closed`; for a stream served under `dao.stream.remote.md`, the\\\\n292-`not-found` protocol error its mirror answers for the reclaimed identity. A remote holder that\\\\n293-reads the grantor's stream through a served handle is observing the grantor's\\\\n294-record, not receiving carriage. No close code is assigned here; distinguishing\\\\n295-reclaim on the wire belongs to `dao.stream.ws.md`'s deferred close-code design.\\\\n296-\\\\n297-## Out of scope\\\\n298-\\\\n299-- **Delegated renewal.** A renewal is evidence about its author.\\\\n300:- **Transfer of a lease between holders.**\\\\n301-- **Who may be granted anything at all**, and **what is leasable** — this\\\\n302-  contract gates nothing, and the domain decides. `dao.stream.remote.md`\\\\n303-  names two subjects: a stream entry served for a remote party, and a relay\\\\n304-  pair on a meeting peer.\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/dao-stream/1790497500000-architect-pending-link-policy-brief.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:18:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_8\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-27 08:24:44 GMT\\\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-27 15:24:44 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\\\\n\\\\n## Pending-link failure policy — owner decision brief\\\\n\\\\n**Decision:** Who ends a link when its content source never answers? M5 currently leaves the link pending without a deadline. At the REPL, the pending run retains subsequent input until the link completes or the caller uses `(abandon)`.\\\\n\\\\n| Option | Decision and change | Cost |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| **A — Keep the current behavior permanently** | The linker stays clock-free. A caller that wants a deadline composes `dao.lease` and calls `abandon` when its lease lapses. | No linker work now; every caller owns its timeout policy indefinitely. |\\\\n| **B — Add a lease per pending link now** | Wire lease grants, renewals, ticks, judging, and reclaim into linker operation. | A new dependency and lease bookkeeping for every pending link, including callers that want to wait indefinitely. |\\\\n| **C — Defer, with a documented composition hook** | Keep M5 behavior and identify the point where a caller’s lease judge would call `abandon`. Implement a deadline when a deployment needs one. | No lease runtime now; documentation and a later implementation decision remain. |\\\\n\\\\n**Current implementation:** M5 implements A’s *behavior*, but the owner has not chosen A as the permanent policy. The [linker design](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md>) assigns retry, deadline, and permanent absence to the composition; `fetch` has no deadline. Its section 6.3 already describes the lease seam: the caller renews on progress, and a judge supplied with ticks calls `abandon` on silence. The [lease contract](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md>) requires that judge and its resource reclaim action to live in the possessing composition. A proposed [REPL link-policy design](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md>) describes a configurable hook, but marks it unimplemented.\\\\n\\\\n**Recommendation: C.** Preserve the clock-free linker and document the caller-side `abandon` seam as the decision for now. This follows the existing ownership boundary and avoids imposing lease state on every link before a deployment supplies a deadline, cadence, and recovery needs. When such a deployment exists, its requirements can determine whether a caller-side lease composition suffices. The owner need only rule now that indefinite pending remains the default and that deadlines belong to the composition.\\\\n\\\\nStatus: COMPLETE\\\"}}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:15:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short --branch'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"## p7b-ticks...origin/p7b-ticks\\n?? collab/yang-php/1794566777000-engineer-p7b-ticks-b.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log\\n?? collab/yang-php/1794566777000-engineer-p7b-ticks-b.prompt.md\\n?? collab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log\\n?? collab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.prompt.md\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:17:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '3500,3640p' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc; cat src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/safepoint.cljc; cat src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/publication.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"  \\\"`[name spec-node]` of the `classDeclaration` or `enumDeclaration` `n`.\\\"\\n  [ctx n]\\n  (if (p/rule? n \\\"enumDeclaration\\\") (enum-spec ctx n) (class-spec* ctx n)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- class-spec*\\n  [ctx0 n]\\n  (let [ks (core/kids ctx0 n)\\n        cname (ns-prefixed ctx0 (leftmost-token-text ctx0 (first (core/rules ctx0 n \\\"identifier\\\"))))\\n        ctx (assoc ctx0 :cname cname)\\n        interface? (boolean (some #(and (p/token? %) (= \\\"Interface\\\" (:type %))) ks))\\n        entry-type (some-> (first (core/rules ctx n \\\"classEntryType\\\")) (->> (leftmost-token-text ctx))\\n                           str/lower-case)\\n        kind (cond interface? :interface (= \\\"trait\\\" entry-type) :trait :else :class)\\n        ctx (assoc ctx :ckind kind)\\n        mods (set (keep #(when (p/rule? % \\\"modifier\\\") (keyword (str/lower-case (leftmost-token-text ctx %))))\\n                        ks))\\n        parent (when-not interface?\\n                 (some->> (drop-while #(not (and (p/token? %) (= \\\"Extends\\\" (:type %)))) ks)\\n                          second\\n                          (class-text ctx)))\\n        interfaces (cond\\n                     interface? (type-names ctx (first (core/rules ctx n \\\"interfaceList\\\")))\\n                     :else (type-names ctx (first (core/rules ctx n \\\"interfaceList\\\"))))\\n        statements (core/rules ctx n \\\"classStatement\\\")\\n        _ (doseq [st statements]\\n            (when (seq (core/rules ctx st \\\"attributes\\\")) (unsupported! st :attribute)))\\n        tok (fn [st] (some #(when (p/token? %) (:type %)) (core/kids ctx st)))\\n        consts (mapcat #(const-entries ctx cname %) (filter #(= \\\"Const\\\" (tok %)) statements))\\n        props (mapcat (fn [st]\\n                        (cond\\n                          (seq (core/rules ctx st \\\"propertyModifiers\\\")) (property-entries ctx cname st)\\n                          (and (= \\\"Function_\\\" (tok st))\\n                               (= \\\"__construct\\\" (str/lower-case (function-written ctx st))))\\n                          (promoted-entries ctx st)\\n                          :else []))\\n                      statements)\\n        methods (mapv #(method-entry ctx % %) (filter #(= \\\"Function_\\\" (tok %)) statements))\\n        uses (filter #(= \\\"Use\\\" (tok %)) statements)\\n        [traits rules] (trait-rules ctx uses)]\\n    [cname\\n     (spec-node (cond-> {:name (u/lit cname),\\n                         :kind (u/lit kind),\\n                         :abstract? (u/lit (boolean (:abstract mods))),\\n                         :final? (u/lit (boolean (:final mods))),\\n                         :interfaces (args-vector (map u/lit interfaces)),\\n                         :traits (args-vector (map u/lit traits)),\\n                         :trait-rules rules,\\n                         :consts (args-vector consts),\\n                         :props (args-vector props),\\n                         :methods (args-vector methods)}\\n                  parent (assoc :parent (u/lit parent))))]))\\n\\n\\n(defn- lower-declare\\n  \\\"`declare(strict_types=1)` switches the unit to strict scalar typing; `ticks`\\n   and `encoding` are accepted and inert. A body statement runs after.\\\"\\n  [ctx n]\\n  (let [ds (mapcat #(core/rules ctx % \\\"directive\\\") (core/rules ctx n \\\"declareList\\\"))\\n        strict (some (fn [d]\\n                       (when (some #(and (p/token? %) (= \\\"StrictTypes\\\" (:type %))) (core/kids ctx d))\\n                         (= \\\"1\\\" (leftmost-token-text ctx (first (core/rules ctx d \\\"numericConstant\\\"))))))\\n                     ds)\\n        body (first (core/rules ctx n \\\"statement\\\"))]\\n    (u/then (if (some? strict) (app* 'php/set-strict! (u/lit (boolean strict))) (u/lit :php/null))\\n            (if body (lower ctx body) (u/lit :php/null)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- lower-class\\n  \\\"A class declared in place (nested or conditional): declared when reached.\\\"\\n  [ctx n]\\n  (let [[_ spec] (class-spec ctx n)]\\n    (u/then (app* 'php/declare-class! spec) (u/lit :php/null))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- lower\\n  \\\"The node of CST rule node `n`.\\\"\\n  [ctx n]\\n  (case (:rule n)\\n    \\\"inlineHtml\\\" (html-node ctx n)\\n    \\\"phpBlock\\\" (sequence-node (block-nodes ctx n))\\n    (\\\"topStatement\\\" \\\"innerStatement\\\" \\\"statement\\\" \\\"namespaceStatement\\\")\\n    (lower ctx (first (core/rule-kids ctx n)))\\n    \\\"globalConstantDeclaration\\\" (lower-const ctx n)\\n    \\\"echoStatement\\\" (with-temps ctx n\\n                      (sequence-node (echo-nodes ctx (first (core/rules ctx n \\\"expressionList\\\")))))\\n    \\\"expressionStatement\\\" (with-temps ctx n (lower ctx (first (core/rules ctx n \\\"expression\\\"))))\\n    \\\"emptyStatement_\\\" (u/lit :php/null)\\n    \\\"inlineHtmlStatement\\\" (sequence-node (block-nodes ctx n))\\n    \\\"blockStatement\\\" (lower ctx (first (core/rules ctx n \\\"innerStatementList\\\")))\\n    \\\"innerStatementList\\\" (sequence-node (block-nodes ctx n))\\n    \\\"expression\\\" (lower-expression ctx n)\\n    \\\"yieldExpression\\\" (lower-yield ctx n)\\n    \\\"parentheses\\\" (lower ctx (first (core/rule-kids ctx n)))\\n    \\\"ifStatement\\\" (lower-if ctx n)\\n    \\\"whileStatement\\\" (lower-while ctx n)\\n    \\\"doWhileStatement\\\" (lower-do-while ctx n)\\n    \\\"forStatement\\\" (lower-for ctx n)\\n    \\\"foreachStatement\\\" (lower-foreach ctx n)\\n    \\\"switchStatement\\\" (lower-switch ctx n)\\n    \\\"breakStatement\\\" (lower-escape ctx n :break)\\n    \\\"continueStatement\\\" (lower-escape ctx n :continue)\\n    \\\"returnStatement\\\" (lower-return ctx n)\\n    \\\"tryCatchFinally\\\" (lower-try ctx n)\\n    \\\"throwStatement\\\" (app* 'php/throw-value (lower ctx (first (core/rules ctx n \\\"expression\\\"))))\\n    \\\"staticVariableStatement\\\" (lower-static ctx n)\\n    \\\"globalStatement\\\" (lower-global ctx n)\\n    \\\"unsetStatement\\\" (lower-unset ctx n (core/rules ctx (first (core/rules ctx n \\\"chainList\\\")) \\\"chain\\\"))\\n    \\\"functionDeclaration\\\" (lower-function ctx n)\\n    \\\"constant\\\" (lower-constant ctx n)\\n    \\\"arrayCreation\\\" (lower-array ctx n)\\n    (\\\"classDeclaration\\\" \\\"enumDeclaration\\\") (lower-class ctx n)\\n    \\\"declareStatement\\\" (lower-declare ctx n)\\n    (core/default-arm diagnostic-ns \\\"PHP\\\" unsupported-rules n)))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Packets\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- hoisted?\\n  \\\"True for a top-level statement that is an unconditional function\\n   declaration: PHP declares it before the unit's first statement runs.\\\"\\n  [ctx item]\\n  (p/rule? (rule-head ctx item) \\\"functionDeclaration\\\"))\\n\\n\\n(defn- declared-functions\\n  \\\"Every function the unit declares: lower-cased fully qualified name to the\\n   vector of its by-reference parameter positions.\\\"\\n  [ctx]\\n  (letfn [(refs\\n            [n]\\n            (let [fps (core/rules ctx (first (core/rules ctx n \\\"formalParameterList\\\"))\\n                                  \\\"formalParameter\\\")]\\n              (vec (keep-indexed (fn [i fp] (when (some #(p/token? % \\\"&\\\") (core/kids ctx fp)) i))\\n                                 fps))))\\n          (walk\\n            [ictx n]\\n            (into (if (p/rule? n \\\"functionDeclaration\\\")\\n                    {(str/lower-case (ns-prefixed ictx (function-written ctx n))) (refs n)}\\n(ns yang.php.antlr.safepoint\\n  \\\"The PHP `:loop` and `:call` hooks. A bounded run charges one safepoint to\\n   its per-run counter; exhaustion unwinds past guest handlers and raises the\\n   operational fatal, not a guest Throwable.\\\"\\n  (:require\\n    [yang.core.uast :as u]))\\n\\n\\n(def profile\\n  {:loop 'php.sp/loop, :call {:hook 'php.sp/call, :sequence true}})\\n\\n\\n(def fatal-text\\n  \\\"Maximum execution time of 30 seconds exceeded\\\")\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private function-definitions\\n  '[[php.sp/exhaust!\\n     ;; the terminal root escape: guest handlers, returns and finalizers\\n     ;; cannot replace the operational fatal\\n     (fn []\\n       (php/root-fatal \\\"Maximum execution time of 30 seconds exceeded\\\"))]\\n    [php.sp/tick\\n     (fn []\\n       (let [n (cell/get php.sp/remaining)]\\n         (if (= n 0)\\n           (php.sp/exhaust!)\\n           (do (cell/set! php.sp/remaining (- n 1)) :php/null))))]\\n    [php.sp/loop (fn [] (php.sp/tick))]\\n    [php.sp/call (fn [] (php.sp/tick))]])\\n\\n\\n(defn- definitions->uast\\n  [defs]\\n  (u/seq-nodes (map (fn [[k form]] (u/def! k (u/sexp->uast form))) defs)))\\n\\n\\n(defn program\\n  \\\"The bounded run: hook definitions and a counter holding `budget`, then the\\n   safepoint-derived program `ast`. `budget` must be a non-negative integer;\\n   anything else is a named host-side error, not a guest diagnostic.\\\"\\n  [budget ast]\\n  (when-not (and (integer? budget) (not (neg? budget)))\\n    (throw (ex-info \\\"php.sp/remaining budget must be a non-negative integer\\\"\\n                    {:yang.php.antlr.safepoint/error :invalid-budget\\n                     :budget budget})))\\n  (u/mark-tails\\n    (u/then (definitions->uast function-definitions)\\n            (u/then (u/def! 'php.sp/remaining (u/sexp->uast (list 'cell/new budget)))\\n                    ast))))\\n(ns yang.php.antlr.publication\\n  \\\"P6 (docs/design/yang.php.md section 11): a set of compiled PHP units\\n   published as linker modules. The ordering, SCC bundling and publish loop\\n   are `yang.core.publication`'s; this namespace supplies the PHP hooks: the\\n   includes `lower/imported-unit-names` folds, the `lower/module-spec` and\\n   `lower/scc-module-specs` emitters, no site set and no binder table (the\\n   PHP hook profile is P7).\\n\\n   A unit is `{:name path :packet p :strict-types b}`, `path` its normalized\\n   relative path; the optional class map entry is `{:name \\\\\\\"$classmap\\\\\\\"\\n   :classes {class path} :paths [path ...]}`. Name assertions stay the\\n   linker's: this namespace publishes content, it signs nothing.\\\"\\n  (:require\\n    [yang.core.publication :as core]\\n    [yang.php.antlr.lower :as lower]\\n    [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]))\\n\\n\\n(defn- class-map?\\n  \\\"The autoload class map entry of a unit set, `{:name \\\\\\\"$classmap\\\\\\\" :classes\\n   {class path} :paths [path ...]}`: it imports every path it lists, so it\\n   publishes last and an unpublished path is refused by name (plan P6 R9).\\\"\\n  [unit]\\n  (= lower/classmap-name (:name unit)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- config\\n  [publish-fn]\\n  {:imported-names (fn [{:keys [name packet], :as unit}]\\n                     (if (class-map? unit)\\n                       (lower/classmap-paths unit)\\n                       (lower/imported-unit-names packet {:path name}))),\\n   :module-spec (fn [registry {:keys [name packet strict-types], :as unit} php-address deps]\\n                  (if (class-map? unit)\\n                    (lower/classmap-spec registry unit php-address deps)\\n                    (lower/module-spec registry packet\\n                                       {:path name,\\n                                        :strict-types strict-types,\\n                                        :php-address php-address,\\n                                        :deps deps}))),\\n   :scc-module-specs lower/scc-module-specs,\\n   :site-set nil,\\n   :binder-table nil,\\n   :refusal-ns \\\"yang.php\\\",\\n   :publish-fn publish-fn})\\n\\n\\n(defn publication-order\\n  \\\"Dependency-first names, retaining the input order for acyclic units.\\n   Cyclic components appear as sorted member vectors in :order, one\\n   publication step per SCC. An include edge no unit and no `published`\\n   entry supplies is refused `:yang.php/unpublished-import` by name.\\\"\\n  [units published]\\n  (core/publication-order (config publish/publish-module!) units published))\\n\\n\\n(defn publish-units!\\n  \\\"Publish `units` into the content store `handle` over the host registry\\n   `registry`, `php` pinned at `php-address` and each include outside the\\n   set at its address in `published` ({path address}), in\\n   `publication-order`. Answers `{:status :ok :addresses {path address}\\n   :bundles {bundle-symbol address}}`, or the first refusal:\\n   `publication-order`'s before anything is written, else\\n   `publish-module!`'s with a string `:unit`.\\\"\\n  ([handle registry php-address units]\\n   (publish-units! handle registry php-address units {}))\\n  ([handle registry php-address units published]\\n   (publish-units! handle registry php-address units published publish/publish-module!))\\n  ([handle registry php-address units published publish-fn]\\n   (core/publish-units! (config publish-fn) handle registry php-address units published)))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:27:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md | head -100; rg -n 'safepoint-test|ticks-test|linked-tracing-profile' deps.edn collab/yang-php/1794566777000-engineer-p7b-ticks-b.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log | tail -20; git log -1 --oneline\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\ncollab/yang-php/1794567390000-engineer-p7b-ticks-c.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:28:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md | head -100; rg -n 'safepoint-test|ticks-test|linked-tracing-profile' deps.edn collab/yang-php/1794566777000-engineer-p7b-ticks-b.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log | tail -20; git log -1 --oneline\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\n---\\n\\n# BUILD & TEST\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n```sh\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\"name\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below for\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\n#\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\n# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\\n# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\\n# never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\\n# Individual tests run fine on the laptop; suite-scale runs (G1 subsystem sets,\\n# G2 changed sets, full suites, slow tier, nightly) run on shiva/jupiter --\\n# the suite has outgrown the laptop (owner ruling 2026-11-04).\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` runs once daily on the remote hosts (04:00 VN);\\n# a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01;\\n# for a fix branch, the previously-failing tests green IS the merge bar\\n# (owner ruling 2026-11-07; see Gate Ladder G0).\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below).\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\n\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\n```\\n\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\n\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\ncollab/yang-php/1794566777000-engineer-p7b-ticks-b.gpt-6.1-terra.stdout.log:44:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\